---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import TopBar from "../components/TopBar.astro";
import Terminal from "../components/Terminal.astro";
import WorkflowShowcase from "../components/WorkflowShowcase.astro";
import Mascot from "../components/Mascot.astro";
import MascotStories from "../components/MascotStories.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { SITE } from "../config";
import { docsHref } from "../lib/paths";
import "../styles/home.css";
const prebuild = `swallowtail check .
swallowtail scene validate --path res://hello.tscn
swallowtail scene play --mode res://hello.tscn
swallowtail runtime errors
swallowtail scene stop`;
const heroSession = [
  { type: "cmd", text: "swallowtail scene tree" },
  { type: "out", text: "Hello (Node2D) ." },
  { type: "out", text: "└─ Greeting (Label) Greeting" },
  { type: "out", text: "" },
  { type: "cmd", text: "swallowtail check ." },
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head><BaseHead /></head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <TopBar showSidebar={false} />
    <main id="main">
      <section class="st-hero st-wrap">
        <div class="st-hero-copy">
          <p class="st-eyebrow">AUTOMATION FOR GODOT</p>
          <h1>Swallowtail<span>At your service.</span></h1>
          <p class="st-lede">Build, inspect, and playtest your game. Put the repetitive work in a script. Keep the creative decisions yours.</p>
          <p class="st-audience">Your game. Your workflow.</p>
          <div class="st-actions">
            <a class="st-button" href={docsHref("quickstart")}>Start building <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a>
            <a class="st-link" href={SITE.repo}>Explore the source <Icon name="github" size={16} /></a>
          </div>
          <p class="st-command-note">Run <code>swallowtail</code> from your terminal. <a href={docsHref("migration")}>Upgrading from godot-mcp?</a></p>
          <p class="st-requirements">Windows, macOS, Linux · Godot 4.7 target<br />Godot 4.3 through 4.8 compatibility in beta · <a href={docsHref("installation")}>Installation &amp; requirements</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="st-hero-stage">
          <div class="st-hero-terminal"><Terminal title="your project · your terminal" lines={heroSession} /></div>
          <Mascot topic="anime" pose="hero" src="/brand/swallowtail-presenting.png" width={1024} height={1536} />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="st-band">
        <WorkflowShowcase />
      </section>

      <section class="st-wrap st-prebuild" aria-labelledby="prebuild-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="st-eyebrow">PUT IT TO WORK</p>
          <h2 id="prebuild-heading">Before your next build.</h2>
          <p>Check your scripts, validate a saved scene, and inspect errors from a short playtest. Keep the commands with your project so you can repeat the checks after a change.</p>
          <p class="st-footnote">Try this from the <a href={docsHref("quickstart")}>quickstart project</a>, with its editor open and the addon enabled. Use your own saved scene path when you adapt it.</p>
          <a class="st-text-link" href={docsHref("automation")}>Turn it into a CI check <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a>
        </div>
        <div class="st-prebuild-code">
          <Code code={prebuild} lang="sh" title="Run from your project directory" />
          <p class="st-footnote">Read the validation results and returned error list before exporting. This sequence inspects a playtest; a CI gate also needs assertions and failure handling.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="st-band st-mascot-divider">
        <div class="st-wrap st-mascot-rail">
          <Mascot topic="nature" pose="sitting" src="/brand/swallowtail-sitting.png?v=matte" width={1024} height={1536} />
        </div>
        <div class="st-wrap st-interfaces">
          <div><h2>CLI first.<br />MCP when you want it.</h2><p>MCP is a connection option. The automation toolkit works without it.</p></div>
          <div class="st-interface-list">
            <article><h3>Use the CLI</h3><p>Run commands from your terminal, reuse them in scripts, or add them to CI. Read the results directly, or pipe JSON into your next step.</p><a href={docsHref("commands")}>Command reference <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a></article>
            <article><h3>Connect through MCP</h3><p>For clients that use MCP, expose the same command handlers through stdio or the editor's HTTP endpoint. Configure this only if your workflow needs it.</p><a href={docsHref("mcp-setup")}>Optional MCP setup <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a></article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="st-band">
        <div class="st-wrap st-ownership" aria-labelledby="ownership-heading">
          <div><p class="st-eyebrow">KEEP CONTROL OF YOUR WORK</p><h2 id="ownership-heading">Your project stays yours.</h2><p>Work with ordinary Godot scenes, scripts, and resources. Open them in the editor, review the changes in Git, and keep building with the tools you choose.</p></div>
          <div class="st-ownership-details">
            <article><h3>Inspect what changes</h3><p>Your scenes, scripts, and resources remain project files you can read, edit, and version.</p></article>
            <article><h3>Undo supported scene edits</h3><p>Supported scene operations use Godot's editor undo history. Script execution and file changes need their own review and recovery.</p></article>
            <article><h3>Keep a checkpoint</h3><p>Save your work and commit a known working state before broad changes. Review the diff and test the result before keeping it.</p></article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="st-wrap st-engine" aria-labelledby="engine-heading">
        <div class="st-section-intro"><h2 id="engine-heading">Godot already has a CLI.<br />Swallowtail works with it.</h2><p>Godot's built-in command line launches the engine and handles tasks such as imports, scripts, and exports. Swallowtail uses your Godot installation for those jobs and adds an interface to the editor session you already have open.</p></div>
        <div class="st-engine-rows">
          <article><h3>Start with the engine</h3><p>Import assets, check scripts, run tests, and export builds through commands that invoke Godot and report the result.</p><code>swallowtail check .</code></article>
          <article><h3>Work in the live editor</h3><p>Inspect the scene tree, change node properties, and validate saved scenes. Read state back as you work.</p><code>swallowtail scene tree</code></article>
          <article><h3>See what the game does</h3><p>Play a scene, inspect runtime properties, send input, and check errors. Verify behavior in the running game.</p><code>swallowtail runtime tree</code></article>
        </div>
        <p class="st-footnote">Swallowtail uses an installed Godot engine; it does not bundle one. <a href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/editor/command_line_tutorial.html">Godot's command-line reference</a> · <a href={docsHref("how-it-works")}>How Swallowtail works</a></p>
      </section>

      <section class="st-wrap st-comparison" aria-labelledby="comparison-heading">
        <p class="st-eyebrow">HOW IT COMPARES</p>
        <h2 id="comparison-heading">Swallowtail next to<br />Godot AI and Godot MCP Native.</h2>
        <p class="st-comparison-intro">What each tool documents: how you start, what you set up, and what you can run from a terminal, a script, or an MCP client. Sources and the comparison date sit under the table.</p>
        <div class="st-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Tool comparison" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <caption>Choose the workflow that fits your project.</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">What you need</th><th scope="col">Swallowtail</th><th scope="col">Godot AI</th><th scope="col">Godot MCP Native</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Starting point</th><td><strong>Standalone automation CLI + editor addon</strong></td><td>MCP integration for the editor</td><td>MCP editor addon + companion CLI</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Run from your terminal</th><td>Use <code>swallowtail</code> directly, without MCP</td><td>Documented setup uses <code>godot-ai attach</code> to connect an MCP client</td><td>Use <code>gdmcp</code> through its separate local CLI API</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Use without an AI agent</th><td><strong>Yes.</strong> The CLI and scripts need no agent, model, or account</td><td>Its requirements list an MCP client; every documented workflow runs through one</td><td>Yes, through its <code>gdmcp</code> CLI, which the docs present for agents with shell access</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Set up the connection</th><td>Go binary + Godot addon for live commands</td><td>Python server via uv + Godot plugin + MCP client</td><td>Godot addon; prebuilt CLI for terminal use</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Automate a workflow</th><td>Create projects, import, check, test, export, and operate the live editor</td><td>Edit scenes and scripts, run projects and test suites through MCP tools</td><td>Discover tools, run domain commands, and preview or apply command batches</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Inspect a playtest</th><td>Runtime state, input, captures, breakpoints, stepping, and hot reload</td><td>Game state, input sequences, screenshots, and error logs</td><td>Runtime probe, breakpoints, stack and variable inspection, and execution control</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Connect an MCP client</th><td>Optional stdio or editor-hosted HTTP interface</td><td>Documented primary workflow</td><td>MCP interface alongside the separate CLI API</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="st-footnote">Compared against the published documentation on September 7, 2026: <a href="https://github.com/hi-godot/godot-ai">Godot AI</a>, <a href="https://github.com/hi-godot/godot-ai/blob/main/docs/TOOLS.md">its tool reference</a>, <a href="https://github.com/yurineko73/Godot-MCP-Native">Godot MCP Native</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/yurineko73/Godot-MCP-Native/tree/main/cli/gdmcp">its CLI reference</a>. This summarizes documented workflows, not an exhaustive feature audit.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="st-wrap st-story" aria-labelledby="story-heading">
        <div><p class="st-eyebrow">WHY THIS PROJECT EXISTS</p><h2 id="story-heading">I just wanted<br />to build games.</h2><p class="st-story-aside">For my son.<br />For myself.</p></div>
        <div class="st-story-copy">
          <p>After twenty years as a programmer, I wanted to spend more time making games. I don't have the budget to hire a team for every idea, and I don't want to spend ten years on a project at the expense of my health or my family.</p>
          <p>What I wanted was a toolkit that lets me work the way I want on any given day, and that includes using AI as one of the tools. Some days that is a terminal and a script I can run again. Some days it is an agent driving the editor while I review what it changed. Swallowtail gives both the same commands, so the choice stays mine at every step between an idea and something you can actually play.</p>
          <p>I understand the concerns about this technology. You don't have to agree with my choices, and I respect that. I'm here to make games and share a tool, not to fight. Questions and criticism are welcome. Bullying and personal attacks aren't.</p>
          <a class="st-text-link" href={docsHref("on-ai")}>The full note on AI and this project <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="st-band st-start st-mascot-finale">
        <Mascot topic="culture" pose="peeking" src="/brand/swallowtail-peeking.png" width={1536} height={1024} />
        <div class="st-wrap"><h2>There's a game you want to make.</h2><p>Start with a scene. Build from there.</p><a class="st-button" href={docsHref("quickstart")}>Meet the toolkit <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a></div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <MascotStories />
    <footer class="st-wrap st-footer"><span>Swallowtail · MIT licensed</span><a href={SITE.repo}>Source</a><a href={docsHref("on-ai")}>About the project</a><a href={docsHref("changelog")}>Changelog</a></footer>
  </body>
</html>
